{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>But.</b> This is the beginning of the verse: \"we were healing,\" and he was bearing our pains — we were causing him pain, and he would endure." "<b>And we accounted him</b> stricken, from the root of והנה הנגע עמד בעיניו (Leviticus 13:5)." "<b>Stricken by Elohim.</b> Construct state — for HaShem struck him and afflicted him." "Until it would be fitting that the diseases should come upon us, because our Torah is all emptiness, and they came upon Israel, whose Torah is a Torah of truth — and the witness: \"All we like sheep have gone astray.\""], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 53, :notes {3 "Ibn Ezra's argument: the diseases rightfully belonged to the nations (whose Torah is emptiness) yet fell upon Israel (Torah of truth); 'the witness' quotes the next verse (Isaiah 53:6) as proof."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:59:17.890086110Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}